<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anthony Ulrich, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1633">1633</a>–<a href="/years/1714">1714</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Anton Ulrich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anthony Ulrich was Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, co-ruling with his brother from 1685 and alone from 1704. A rival of the Hanoverian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welfs</a>, he was also a novelist, author of long courtly romances, and an art collector whose collections later formed the Herzog Anton Ulrich Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. He arranged his granddaughter's marriage to the future Emperor Charles VI and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1710.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anthony-ulrich-duke-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel.jpg" alt="Anthony Ulrich, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Bernhard Francke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_Bernhard_Francke_-_Herzog_Anton_Ulrich_von_Braunschweig_(1633-1714).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
